(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-parasites-of-medical-importance-quiz"]={"title":"Parasites of Medical Importance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement describes how Strongyloides stercoralis can reinfect the same host?","choices":["Its larvae pass through a snail first","Its eggs are swallowed in food or water","It does so without leaving the body","Its eggs on the anal skin are swallowed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Strongyloides stercoralis, the threadworm, can reinfect the same host without leaving the body. Its larvae normally penetrate the skin, migrate to the lungs, are swallowed and complete development in the intestine; heavy worm loads can cause pneumonitis, eosinophilia, bloody diarrhea and malabsorption. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 80, 91, 94, 98 and 100"},{"q":"Outside the intestine, which organ does Entamoeba histolytica invade most commonly?","choices":["Lungs","Brain","Spleen","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In extraintestinal amebiasis the amebas invade the viscera of the peritoneal cavity, most commonly the liver, where they produce abscesses with active trophozoites (amebic hepatitis). The lungs, spleen, adrenals, kidneys, skin and brain can also be infected. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 21, 29, 46 and 53"},{"q":"What is a host in which a helminth undergoes no parasitic development called?","choices":["A biological vector","A transport host","An intermediate host","A definitive host"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A transport host experiences no parasitic development; it only carries the worm onward. Toxocara's paratenic hosts work this way: larvae sit in the tissues of animals such as waterfowl without growing, and a dog, cat or human that eats the undercooked meat is infected. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 79, 98, 104, 108 and 128"},{"q":"A person who often eats undercooked crustaceans is at risk for which parasite?","choices":["Paragonimus westermani","Opisthorchis sinensis","Fasciola hepatica","Taenia solium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lung fluke Paragonimus westermani cycles between carnivorous animals, snails and crustaceans. Humans are infected by eating undercooked crustaceans, and the worms migrate to the lungs. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 116, 117 and 121"},{"q":"A lifelong resident of the southern United States who has never traveled abroad is diagnosed with Chagas disease. What best explains acquiring it at home?","choices":["Spread of the tsetse fly vector","Dogs serving as alternate hosts","Lake water entering the nose","Range expansion of its vector"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease was a tropical disorder of Latin America, but it is becoming an issue in the southern United States as its vector, the reduviid (kissing) bug, expands its range. Many now consider it endemic in the United States, so a case can be acquired without travel. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 5, 28, 45, 48, 49 and 52"},{"q":"Which group is advised not to clean a cat's litter box because of Toxoplasma gondii?","choices":["Pregnant women","Contact lens wearers","Children in daycare","People without a spleen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cats harbor Toxoplasma oocysts and release them in their feces, and a pregnant woman who acquires toxoplasmosis can transmit it across the placenta to her fetus. So pregnant women should not clean the cat box, because of the danger of transplacental transmission. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 5, 64, 65, 31, 6 and 77"},{"q":"How do humans acquire Taenia saginata?","choices":["Eating raw beef holding encysted larvae","Swallowing eggs from feces-tainted land","Eating cysts in undercooked pork","Handling dogs that carry the worm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Taenia saginata is the beef tapeworm, and humans are its definitive host. Infection occurs from eating raw beef in which the larval form has encysted; in the human, the larva attaches to the small intestine and becomes an adult. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 84, 118, 119, 121, 124 and 125"},{"q":"What produces the regular episodes of chills, fever and sweating in malaria?","choices":["Shifting surface antigens","Infected red blood cells bursting","Sporozoites dividing in the liver","Gametes fusing in the bloodstream"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the erythrocytic phase, merozoites enter red blood cells, become ring trophozoites and multiply until the cell bursts and releases more merozoites. The symptoms recur at intervals as the red blood cells rupture, and the interval depends on the species. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 58, 59, 60 and 46"},{"q":"Where are the cysts of cysticercosis most damaging?","choices":["Bile duct, gallbladder or pancreas","The spleen and the bladder","Heart muscle, eye or brain","The liver and the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In cysticercosis, eggs hatch in the intestine and release larvae that migrate to all tissues and encyst. The cysts are most damaging if they lodge in heart muscle, eye or brain, and in the brain they may cause seizures and psychiatric disturbances. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 113, 117, 121 and 124"},{"q":"Which measure prevents amebiasis caused by Entamoeba histolytica?","choices":["Cooking pork thoroughly","Controlling freshwater snails","Treating the water supply","Using nose plugs when swimming"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Water treatment is the prevention for amebiasis (a vaccine is under development). Entamoeba histolytica cysts are swallowed, and epidemics occur in concentrated populations where water supplies are polluted, such as prisons, hospitals and juvenile care institutions. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 16, 17, 19, 23, 29, 101, 112 and 113"},{"q":"Giardiasis is common in campers. How is Giardia lamblia usually acquired?","choices":["Water splashing up into the nose","Bites of blood-sucking insects","Handling infected farm pigs","Swallowed cysts in water or food"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Giardia lamblia cysts, small, compact and multinucleate, survive in the environment and are usually ingested with water and food; giardiasis is common in campers. The cysts enter the duodenum, germinate, and the trophozoites feed and multiply in the jejunum. Boiling, ozone and iodine kill the agent. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 4, 28, 33, 39, 41 and 42"},{"q":"Which measure controls both African sleeping sickness and Chagas disease?","choices":["Controlling the insect vector","Avoiding undercooked meat","Controlling freshwater snails","Washing fruits and vegetables"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. African sleeping sickness is spread by tsetse flies, and its control involves eliminating the tsetse fly. Chagas disease is spread by the reduviid bug, and its control is through control of the vector; a Chagas vaccine is still under development. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 42, 45, 47, 49, 51, 71, 99, 101, 112 and 113"},{"q":"A previously healthy person develops headache, fever, vomiting and a stiff neck a few days after jet skiing on a lake, then becomes confused and starts hallucinating. How does this infection typically progress?","choices":["Brain symptoms only years later","Chronic heart and brain inflammation","Rapid brain destruction and death","Meningitis with a longer progression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Headache, fever, vomiting and stiff neck after lake water contact, then confusion and hallucinations, is Naegleria meningoencephalitis. It causes rapid destruction of the brain and spinal cord, with lack of balance, coma and death, and its treatment must be early. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 28, 29, 31, 46 and 49"},{"q":"A young, sexually active woman has a foul-smelling, green-to-yellow vaginal discharge, vulvitis, and urinary frequency with pain. How is the suspected protozoan diagnosed?","choices":["Examination of a fecal smear","Card agglutination test","Polymerase chain reaction of stool","Microscopy of a vaginal smear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Foul-smelling, green-to-yellow discharge with vulvitis and painful, frequent urination in a young, sexually active woman points to trichomoniasis, the most common curable sexually transmitted disease in this group. Trichomonas vaginalis is diagnosed by microscopic analysis of a vaginal smear. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 4, 23, 37, 38, 41 and 47"},{"q":"How do humans acquire Opisthorchis sinensis, the Chinese liver fluke?","choices":["Eating undercooked freshwater fish","Eating undercooked freshwater crabs","Eating raw aquatic plants","Eating raw or undercooked pork"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Chinese liver fluke cycles between mammals, snails and fish, and humans are infected by eating inadequately cooked fish that carry its larvae; hence the warning not to eat freshwater sushi. Its eggs leave in the feces, reach water and infect snails. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 84, 116, 117 and 121"},{"q":"Cattle and pigs pick up Sarcocystis cysts while grazing on grass contaminated with human feces, and people are infected by eating their undercooked meat. What role do these animals play in the parasite's life cycle?","choices":["Transport hosts","Dead-end hosts","Intermediate hosts","Definitive hosts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For Sarcocystis, domestic animals are the intermediate hosts: they pick up infective cysts while grazing on grass contaminated with human feces, and humans are infected when the undercooked meat is eaten. Sarcocystis hominis comes from cattle and Sarcocystis suihominis from pigs. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 71, 79 and 108"},{"q":"How do people typically acquire Echinococcus?","choices":["Being bitten by fleas","Eating undercooked crabs","Handling infected dogs","Eating undercooked pork"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. People typically acquire echinococcosis by handling dogs who have the infection; these tiny tapeworms are found in dogs. The disease is most prevalent where dogs interact with the intermediate hosts: sheep, cattle, goats and pigs. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 84, 116, 124, 125 and 130"},{"q":"Adult Toxocara canis worms live and mate in the small intestine of a dog. What is the dog's role in this worm's life cycle?","choices":["Intermediate host","Paratenic host","Accidental host","Definitive host"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adulthood and mating occur in the definitive host, and adult Toxocara live in the small intestine of the dog (Toxocara canis) or the cat (Toxocara cati), which pass the eggs in their feces. Humans, by contrast, are accidental hosts, infected by swallowing those eggs. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 79, 98, 108 and 109"},{"q":"How is Onchocerca volvulus, the cause of river blindness, transmitted?","choices":["By the bite of black flies","By the bite of mosquitoes","By the bite of tsetse flies","By the bite of deer ticks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Onchocerca volvulus is transmitted by biting black flies, which act as both vector and intermediate host. Larvae develop into adults in subcutaneous tissues, adult females migrate via the blood to the eyes, and coinfection with Wolbachia bacteria causes river blindness. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 45, 47, 77, 104 and 106"},{"q":"Which is one way helminths cause disease in their human hosts?","choices":["Provoking immune attack on their cuticle","Multiplying inside red blood cells","Growing within the host cells they infect","Feeding on and migrating through tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Helminth pathology arises from worms feeding on and migrating through tissues, from the accumulation of worms in organs, causing blockages, and from toxic worm secretions. The effects are enlargement and swelling of organs, hemorrhage, weight loss and anemia. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 56, 58, 77, 79 and 85"},{"q":"Which drawback do antiprotozoan drugs share as a group?","choices":["Loss of effect from antigenic variation","Toxicity and severe side effects","No activity against eukaryotic cells","Resistance in most protozoan strains"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antiprotozoan drugs have toxicity problems and severe side effects. They work against eukaryotic cells (protozoa are eukaryotic parasites), and they are relatively few in comparison with antibacterial agents, so both their number and their safety are limited. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 9, 12, 14, 38, 46 and 61"},{"q":"By which route is Isospora belli transmitted to humans?","choices":["The bite of a blacklegged deer tick","Undercooked beef or pork","Sexual contact with a partner","Fecally contaminated food or drink"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isospora belli, an intracellular intestinal parasite with an oocyst stage, is transmitted in fecally contaminated food or drink. Infection is usually asymptomatic or self-limited and is primarily associated with immunocompromised hosts. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 74, 77, 71 and 37"},{"q":"What is the only host of the whipworm, Trichuris trichiura?","choices":["Humans","Pigs and wild mammals","Dogs and cats","Freshwater snails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Humans are the sole host of the whipworm. Its eggs, spread by fecal pollution of soil, are swallowed and hatch in the intestine, where the larvae attach, penetrate the outer wall and develop into adults. Trichuriasis has its highest incidence in the tropics, and in the United States in the South. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 8, 84, 93, 98, 108 and 109"},{"q":"Which form of leishmaniasis is the most severe and fatal if untreated?","choices":["Primary chancre","Kala azar","Baghdad boil","Espundia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leishmaniasis takes cutaneous, mucous membrane and systemic (visceral) forms, and kala azar is the most severe form, fatal if untreated. The cutaneous oriental sore, or Baghdad boil, is a localized ulcerated sore, and espundia is a chronic infection of the head. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 5, 46, 52 and 53"},{"q":"Which worm disease is the most common among children in temperate zones?","choices":["Toxocariasis (dog roundworm)","Enterobiasis (pinworm)","Trichuriasis (whipworm)","Ascariasis (roundworm)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enterobiasis, the pinworm or seatworm infection, is the most common worm disease of children in temperate zones. It is very common in daycare, spreading by direct contact and fomites; swallowed eggs hatch in the small intestine, and females emerge to release eggs, causing anal itching. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 6, 10, 91, 93, 94 and 108"},{"q":"Which finding distinguishes rat lungworm meningitis from bacterial meningitis?","choices":["Encysted larvae on muscle biopsy","Eosinophils on lumbar puncture","Neutrophils on lumbar puncture","Headache, stiff neck and vomiting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angiostrongylus cantonensis produces the symptoms of bacterial meningitis (nausea, vomiting, headaches, stiff neck), but the lumbar puncture shows elevated eosinophils instead of neutrophils. That is why the disease is called eosinophilic meningitis; eosinophils are the defense against worms. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 7, 86, 98, 99, 101 and 102"},{"q":"A swimmer develops diarrhea, vomiting and abdominal cramps after swimming in a properly chlorinated public pool. Which protozoan is the most likely cause?","choices":["Balantidium coli","Cryptosporidium","Naegleria fowleri","Acanthamoeba"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cryptosporidium is the leading cause of recreational waterborne disease and can tolerate the chlorine in swimming pools. Swallowed oocysts release sporozoites that penetrate intestinal cells, causing gastroenteritis with headache, sweating, vomiting, abdominal cramps and diarrhea. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 72, 73, 24, 29, 31 and 33"},{"q":"Apart from the bite of its insect vector, by which route can a person acquire Plasmodium?","choices":["Receiving a blood transfusion","Eating undercooked meat","Nasal contact with lake water","Swallowing oocysts in pool water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Besides the bite of the female Anopheles mosquito, its primary vector, Plasmodium is transmitted by blood transfusions and from mother to fetus. That fits a parasite whose erythrocytic phase lives inside red blood cells, where merozoites become ring trophozoites and multiply. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 56, 58, 64, 66, 71, 28, 29, 72 and 73"},{"q":"Which diseases do fleas carry as they feed on warm-blooded animals?","choices":["Malaria and filariasis","Lyme disease and tularemia","Plague and murine typhus","Epidemic typhus and relapsing fever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fleas are highly motile insects with flattened bodies that feed on warm-blooded animals and carry zoonotic diseases: plague and murine typhus. Like other arthropods of medical importance, they transmit the microbes in the process of feeding, which makes them biological vectors. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 128, 129 and 130"},{"q":"As Trypanosoma brucei multiplies in the blood, which organs does it damage?","choices":["Liver, lungs and adrenals","Duodenum and jejunum","Spleen, lymph nodes and brain","Heart muscle and large intestine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After the bite, Trypanosoma brucei migrates into the lymphatics, disseminates in the blood, multiplies there and damages the spleen, lymph nodes and brain. Early fever, enlarged spleen and swollen lymph nodes give way to personality and behavioral changes, then sleep disturbances, seizures and coma. — Lecture 16 Dr. Webster Parasites of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 21, 39, 41, 46, 47 and 49"}]};
